## 6. Design Rationale

### 6.1 Why These Typography Choices?

**Google Sans Flex** provides:

- Modern, friendly aesthetic that pairs beautifully with baby pink
- Variable font technology for flexible weight adjustments
- Excellent legibility at small sizes
- Professional yet approachable character

**Base size of 16px** because:

- Optimal for reading on screens
- Accessibility standard
- Most UI elements work at this size without scaling
- Browser default (1rem)

**±2px increments** because:

- Minimal visual hierarchy needed
- Prevents size chaos
- Relies on weight + color for differentiation
- Creates consistent rhythm

### 6.2 Why This Color Approach?

**Baby pink as primary** creates:

- Soft, approachable brand personality
- High contrast against dark neutrals
- Distinctive, memorable UI
- Versatile for both modes

**OKLCH as default** because:

- Perceptually uniform (unlike HSL)
- More predictable lightness
- Better saturation control (chroma)
- Industry standard (Tailwind v4)
- Future-proof

**Lightness-based hierarchy** because:

- Human perception responds to brightness
- Creates natural elevation system
- Works across all colors consistently
- Reduces cognitive load

### 6.3 Visual Hierarchy Logic

**Primary Information:**

- Larger size (text-lg to text-2xl)
- Heavier weight (semibold to bold)
- Higher contrast (90-100% lightness in dark mode)
- Example: Page titles, card headings, primary CTAs

**Secondary Information:**

- Base size (text-base)
- Medium weight (normal to medium)
- Medium contrast (70% lightness in dark mode)
- Example: Body text, descriptions, UI labels

**Tertiary Information:**

- Smaller size (text-sm to text-xs)
- Normal weight
- Lower contrast (55% lightness in dark mode)
- Example: Timestamps, metadata, helper text

#### The Rule: Only ONE element should be "shouting" at any given moment. Use size + weight + color in combination, not isolation

